art kindergarten and it meant I\\u0027d have to entrust his care to some stranger. Not that school nurses aren\\u0027t capable and wonderful people, it wouldn\\u0027t have mattered their credibility, I\\u0027d have been scared nonetheless. I voiced these concerns to the principal at the time and was assured that I could set a meeting with their nurse well ahead of time, to put my mind at ease. I was nervous, but grateful for their understanding. Then I met (we\\u0027ll call her nurse 1). She was AMAZING! Sweet, kind, patient, compassionate... all the things you\\u0027d truly want in anyone responsible for your child\\u0027s well-being in your stead. She called me frequently, daily, with updates and reports. She kept in great communication. My son\\u0027s diabetes was very well- controlled and best of all, my son adored her! She was the first person, outside of his mother, to help him with his diabetes. A daily struggle for him and whether he\\u0027d have been able to voice it or not, a situation that must have also made him anxious and scared. They clicked so well. A bond that, combined with an amazing kindergarten teacher who also handled the situation fabulously, that made his first year of school, a complete and total success! At the end of the year, tearful goodbyes were exchanged because my son was devastated he wouldn\\u0027t see them ALL summer. He was comforted by the fact that he\\u0027d see them again next year. He clung to that fact through the entire summer, writing them notes and drawing them pictures, so they\\u0027d see how much he practiced and was improving, and to tell them all about his summer fun! I didn\\u0027t need to schedule another meeting with his nurse prior to Meet The Teacher night for 1 grade, because I already knew she was dynamite! She left. Was told she was NOT allowed to tell ANY of her students she was leaving. How hard it was, but she just couldn\\u0027t stay. Couldn\\u0027t say why. So many unhappy teachers and staff. Why? It used to be great...\",\"reviewerRating\":3,\"lastUpdatedDate\":\"June 2025\",\"institutionGreatschoolId\":402900,\"maponicsId\":4394273,\"url\":\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.greatschools.org\u002Farizona\u002Fgilbert\u002F2900-Power-Ranch-Elementary-School\u002F\"},{\"schoolId\":43352811,\"reviewedBy\":\"Parent\",\"datePosted\":\"April 2018\",\"review\":\"I have two kids that attend Power Ranch. Love this school and everything they represent.\",\"reviewerRating\":4,\"lastUpdatedDate\":\"June 2025\",\"institutionGreatschoolId\":402900,\"maponicsId\":3353944,\"url\":\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.greatschools.org\u002Farizona\u002Fgilbert\u002F2900-Power-Ranch-Elementary-School\u002F\"},{\"schoolId\":43352810,\"reviewedBy\":\"Parent\",\"datePosted\":\"November 2017\",\"review\":\"The teachers and staff are amazing. We love this school\",\"reviewerRating\":5,\"lastUpdatedDate\":\"June 2025\",\"institutionGreatschoolId\":402900,\"maponicsId\":3012624,\"url\":\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.greatschools.org\u002Farizona\u002Fgilbert\u002F2900-Power-Ranch-Elementary-School\u002F\"},{\"schoolId\":43352809,\"reviewedBy\":\"Parent\",\"datePosted\":\"July 2016\",\"review\":\"My daughter excelled here every year. The teachers and administration care abou the students and build community that can\\u0027t be beat. I loved the neighborhood feel and the fact that kids walk and ride their bicycles to school, they still say the Pledge of Allegience and parents are very involved - from volunteering, to working at the school and helping with the various events. It\\u0027s a great school! Believe in your community school, involve yourself in your community school and it becomes a great place.\",\"reviewerRating\":5,\"lastUpdatedDate\":\"June 2025\",\"institutionGreatschoolId\":402900,\"maponicsId\":2204256,\"url\":\"https:\u002F\u002Fwww.greatschools.org\u002Farizona\u002Fgilbert\u002F2900-Power-Ranch-Elementary-School\u002F\"},{\"schoolId\":43352808,\"reviewedBy\":\"Parent\",\"datePosted\":\"May 2016\",\"rev